description:
Add a `profile.rs` module to `data-dict-parquet` that computes per-column summaries of a parquet file. This is the shared engine behind the upcoming `describe` (#146) and `draft` (#147) commands, which are just two views over it.

```rust
pub struct ColumnProfile {
    pub row_count: usize,
    pub null_count: usize,
    pub distinct: Distinct,        // Exact(n) | Approx(n) once the tracked-value cap is hit
    pub min: Option<Value>,        // ordered types only
    pub max: Option<Value>,
    pub value_counts: Vec<(Value, usize)>,  // top-k, k = 20
    pub histogram: Option<Histogram>,       // numeric/temporal
    pub examples: Vec<Value>,      // 5 evenly spaced along sorted uniques
}

pub struct Histogram {
    /// 20 equal-width bins spanning [min, max]. Nulls are never counted
    /// here — that's `null_count`, which applies to every type.
    pub bins: Vec<Bin>,
    /// NaN values, for float columns only (NaN is neither null nor orderable,
    /// so it gets its own bin).
    pub nan_count: usize,
}

/// Counts values in (lower, upper], or [lower, upper] when `lower_inclusive`.
/// Only the first bin is lower-inclusive, so the minimum has a home — but the
/// flag is carried on the bin, not left as a convention, so every consumer
/// (renderer, JSON) sees the boundary rule explicitly.
pub struct Bin {
    pub lower: Value,
    pub upper: Value,
    pub lower_inclusive: bool,
    pub count: usize,
}

/// Per-column accumulator for distinct values and their counts.
struct ValueCounts {
    /// Occurrence counts for up to 1,000 tracked values. Once full,
    /// already-tracked values keep counting (their totals stay exact), but
    /// unseen values are no longer added.
    counts: HashMap<Value, usize>,
    /// Set when a new value arrives after `counts` is full, i.e. at least one
    /// distinct value went untracked.
    saturated: bool,
    /// Every value, tracked or not, is fed to this sketch; it answers the
    /// distinct count once the map saturates.
    sketch: HyperLogLog,
}
```

`distinct`, `value_counts`, and `examples` all derive from `ValueCounts`:

- unsaturated → `Distinct::Exact(counts.len())`, exact top-20, exact examples.
- saturated → `Distinct::Approx(sketch.estimate())`. The 1k cap only bounds the *tracked* values; the HyperLogLog sketch counts distinct values without bound in ~16 KB per column (precision 14, ~1% typical error). The top-20 and examples come from the tracked values only — tracked counts are still exact totals, but a value first seen *after* saturation is invisible, so a late-arriving heavy hitter can be missed — both are marked approximate.
- HLL is ~100 lines to hand-roll; per the benchmarking guidance, measure a candidate crate (binary size, deps, build time) against hand-rolling before taking the dependency.

The dictionary fast path feeds the same structure (counting dictionary indices, then resolving through the page), so the cap and saturation semantics are identical on both paths.

Design:

- **Single streaming pass** per file with per-column accumulators, following the existing `column_stats` pattern. Accept an optional column filter so `describe <file> <column>` only reads one column.
- **Dictionary-page fast path** (as in D03/uniqueness): dict-encoded columns get exact distinct counts and value counts from dictionary pages + index counting, without decoding values.
- **Fallback** for plain-encoded columns: decode values into the same `ValueCounts` accumulator, with the cap and saturation semantics above.
- **Histogram**: 20 bins between min and max taken from footer statistics so bins are known before the pass; fall back to a second pass when stats are absent. Nulls are never binned (`null_count` covers every type); NaNs in float columns get their own bin.
- **Examples**: the spec's recommended baseline — 5 evenly spaced values along the sorted unique values, drawn from the same capped (≤ 1k) distinct set.
- Expose whatever type metadata downstream needs that `column_type_info` doesn't already carry (notably timestamp `isAdjustedToUTC`, for `time_zone` inference in `draft`).

Tests: generated parquet fixtures covering dict-encoded vs plain columns, multi row-group files, files without footer statistics, and every dict type (number, string, boolean, date, datetime).
